# CODEOWNERS — review required on the trusted computing base.
#
# WHY THESE PATHS AND NOT THE WHOLE REPO.
#
# Every verdict this repository produces — "the spec is strong", "the proof
# kernel-checked", "the gate passed" — is produced BY code in this repository.
# The paths below are the code that decides what counts as verified. They are
# not verified by anything else, because nothing else is left: the checker
# cannot check itself, and `scripts/gate_integrity.py` says so in its own
# docstring. That is the root of trust, and the root of trust has exactly one
# defence, which is a human reading the diff.
#
# The failure this prevents is silent, not loud. Weakening a threshold in
# ci/gates.toml, deleting a check inside a verifier, or dropping a required
# context from a workflow does not turn anything red. It turns everything
# green — for the wrong reason. Every downstream gate keeps reporting PASS,
# and every PASS now means less than it did before the edit, with nothing in
# the run output to say so. A change to any path below can silently weaken
# every other check in the system.
#
# The corpus is here for the same reason. specs/, proofs/ and semantics/ are
# the statements being proved. A spec edited to claim less is still provable,
# still green, and no longer says what it said. That is not a code change with
# a test to catch it; it is a change to what the tests mean.
#
# requirements.txt / requirements.lock are here because a verifier is only as
# trustworthy as the tool it shells out to. Swapping the pinned bandit,
# pyright, mutmut or axle changes the verdict without changing one line of the
# code that reports it.
#
# ENFORCEMENT — READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE.
#
# This file, on its own, enforces nothing. GitHub only blocks a merge on an
# unreviewed owned path when the branch ruleset requires code-owner review.
# The live ruleset for this repository ("final countdown protection",
# id 20990225) currently sets `require_code_owner_review: false`, which makes
# every rule below advisory: reviewers get requested, and a merge without them
# still succeeds. Turning that flag on is an owner action against the live
# ruleset and is deliberately not done from inside the repo — a tree that can
# arm its own enforcement can also disarm it.
#
# Last matching pattern wins, so order is significant: the broad directory
# rules come first and nothing below them narrows the set.

# Fallback. Every file has an owner unless a later rule overrides.
*                       @Intellora-ai

# CI configuration and the workflows that run every gate. A deleted step, an
# `if:` that never fires, or a `continue-on-error` leaves the job green and the
# gate gone.
/.github/               @Intellora-ai

# Every verifier and gate, including the integrity checker that guards them.
# The last line of defence against a weakened threshold is reading this diff.
/scripts/               @Intellora-ai

# The canonical verification topology: which gates exist, which are mandatory,
# and which contexts GitHub must require. Four consumers read it and nothing
# else declares the same facts.
/ci/                    @Intellora-ai

# The formal corpus — the statements being proved, and their proofs. A spec
# edited to claim less still verifies.
/specs/                 @Intellora-ai
/proofs/                @Intellora-ai
/semantics/             @Intellora-ai

# Dependency surface. The verdict depends on the version of the tool that
# produced it.
requirements.txt        @Intellora-ai
requirements.lock       @Intellora-ai

# This file. Ownership of the ownership rules is itself a protected path;
# without this line, the first edit to CODEOWNERS could remove every line
# above it.
/.github/CODEOWNERS     @Intellora-ai
